## Cursor Rule: Supabase Two-Project Workflow With Migration Files

- **Scope**: Applies to all Supabase-related work in this workspace, including database schema changes, SQL, auth, storage, edge functions, RLS, logs, and project inspection.
- **Model**: Since 2026-08-18 (decision `docs/decisions/0003-dev-prod-environment-split.md`) there are two Supabase projects. Production `jbgaqpdjauzoocplgdsn` serves real customers and the deployed Vercel app. Dev `mbkkzuqjbdvzelqvuzcn` ("WerkFlow App Dev") serves `.env.local`, local development, and the entire Playwright harness. Schemas are kept identical through the committed migration history in `supabase/migrations/`. The operational reference is `docs/technical/environments.md`.

### The migration rule

- Every schema change is **a committed file** in `supabase/migrations/<version>_<name>.sql` — never ad-hoc DDL that exists only in a database.
- Every schema change applies **dev-first, prod-second**: verify on dev (types, tests), then apply the identical SQL to prod via MCP `apply_migration` or the Management API.
- Never `supabase link` or `db push` against prod; the repo is CLI-linked to the dev ref only.
- After schema changes, regenerate `lib/supabase/database.types.ts` from **dev**: `bunx supabase gen types typescript --project-id mbkkzuqjbdvzelqvuzcn --schema public`.
- The four `*baseline*` repair migrations reconcile pre-split unrecorded drift; they are idempotent no-ops on prod and their history semantics must never be edited.

### Required workflow

- **Live inspection first**: when a task depends on current database or platform state, inspect the real project (MCP tools, CLI, or Management API) instead of guessing from code alone. Prod is the source of truth for production state; dev mirrors it via the migration history, so dev inspection or the migration files answer schema questions equally.
- **Writes belong on dev**: routine schema/SQL/edge-function work targets the dev project. Production writes happen only as the prod-second half of the migration rule.
- **Types stay aligned**: when schema changes affect app code, update the generated types and TypeScript usage in the same change.

### Review checklist (must-pass)

- Schema changes exist as committed migration files and were applied dev-first.
- No `link`/`db push` against prod; no prod-only DDL.
- Database-related claims are grounded in actual Supabase inspection when needed.
- App code and `lib/supabase/database.types.ts` remain aligned with the schema.
